# Registry block media (images & video)

Use this when a block needs raster images (PNG, JPG, WebP) or video (MP4, WebM). Most blocks (navbars, SVG-only sections, etc.) do not need this.

## How it works

Block code hardcodes CDN URLs directly. Consumers install the block and media works immediately — no config, no env vars, no `next.config` changes.

The `mediaOrigin` / `NEXT_PUBLIC_USE_LOCAL_BLOCK_MEDIA` pattern exists only in this repo for contributors who need to develop against local assets. It is **never** shipped in installed block code.

## Consumer install

```bash
npx shadcn@latest add "https://uiception.com/r/<block-id>.json"
```

That's it. CDN images render immediately, zero setup.

**To swap an asset:** the block code is installed in their project — they own it. They change the URL in their copy of the file and drop their file in the pre-created `public/images/blocks/<block-id>/` folder (created by the `.gitkeep` files included in the registry item).

## Authoring: adding media to a block

### 1. Hardcode the CDN URL in the entry file

```tsx
const HERO_BG = "https://uiception.com/images/blocks/hero-section-v1/hero-section-v1-bg.png"

// helper variant (multiple files)
const blockImage = (filename: string) =>
  `https://uiception.com/images/blocks/<block-id>/${filename}`

const blockVideo = (filename: string) =>
  `https://uiception.com/videos/blocks/<block-id>/${filename}`
```

**`next/image`** — always add `unoptimized` so consumers don't need `remotePatterns` in their own `next.config`:

```tsx
<Image src={HERO_BG} unoptimized alt="" fill />
```

**Plain string constant** (shader props, video `src`):

```tsx
const VIDEO_SRC = blockVideo("screen-demo.mp4")
```

### 2. Keep media URLs in the entry file — not in `config.ts`

`config.ts` holds pure data (types, labels, item arrays). URL construction belongs in the entry file.

```ts
// config.ts — pure data only
export const imageFiles = [
  { file: "photo-1.jpg", alt: "Description" },
] as const
```

```tsx
// entry file — URL construction here
const items = imageFiles.map(({ file, alt }) => ({
  src: blockImage(file),
  alt,
}))
```

### 3. Add a `.gitkeep` per asset in `registry.json`

Include one empty `.gitkeep` file per expected asset so the consumer's `public/` folder is pre-created on install. They see exactly which files to replace.

```json
{
  "path": "public/images/blocks/<block-id>/hero-bg.png.gitkeep",
  "type": "registry:file",
  "target": "public/images/blocks/<block-id>/hero-bg.png.gitkeep"
}
```

Create the matching empty file in this repo under `public/images/blocks/<block-id>/`.

### 4. No binary files, no env vars, no extra dependencies

### 5. Place assets in `public/`

```
public/images/blocks/<block-id>/   ← images + .gitkeep files
public/videos/blocks/<block-id>/   ← videos + .gitkeep files
```

## What NOT to do

| ❌ Don't | ✅ Do |
|---|---|
| `mediaOrigin` or env var logic in block code | Hardcode CDN URL — it's the consumer's code to edit |
| `NEXT_PUBLIC_USE_LOCAL_BLOCK_MEDIA` in installed files | Contributor-only — stays in `.env`, never shipped |
| Single `.gitkeep` for the whole folder | One `<asset>.gitkeep` per expected file |
| Put URLs in `config.ts` | Filenames in config; URLs in entry file |
| `next/image` without `unoptimized` on CDN src | Add `unoptimized` — consumers need no `remotePatterns` |
| List binary files in `registry.json` | Only `.tsx`, `.ts`, `.css`, `.gitkeep` source files |
